9. Deploy AKS using Terraform
Step 1 – Authenticate to Azure
az login

az account list --output table

az account set --subscription "<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>"


For AzureRM 5.0.0, make the subscription ID available through the ARM_SUBSCRIPTION_ID environment variable if it is not otherwise configured.

PowerShell:
$env:ARM_SUBSCRIPTION_ID = "<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>"


Step 2 – Initialize Terraform
terraform init


Step 3 – Format configuration
terraform fmt -recursive


Step 4 – Validate configuration
terraform validate


Step 5 – Generate execution plan
terraform plan -var-file="terraform.tfvars" -out="aks.tfplan"


Step 6 – Create AKS cluster
terraform apply aks.tfplan


AKS provisioning can take several minutes. Azure will provision the managed control plane and the VM Scale Sets backing the node pools.
10. Connect to the AKS cluster
After deployment, retrieve the Kubernetes credentials:
az aks get-credentials \
  --resource-group rg-kyn-aks \
  --name kyn-aks-cluster


Check the worker nodes:
kubectl get nodes -o wide


Expected output (illustrative):
NAME                                STATUS   ROLES
aks-systempool-xxxxx-vmss000000     Ready    <none>
aks-systempool-xxxxx-vmss000001     Ready    <none>
aks-userpool-xxxxx-vmss000000       Ready    <none>
aks-userpool-xxxxx-vmss000001       Ready    <none>


Check node pools:
az aks nodepool list \
  --resource-group rg-kyn-aks \
  --cluster-name kyn-aks-cluster \
  --output table


Check Kubernetes namespaces and pods:
kubectl get namespaces

kubectl get pods -A

kubectl get nodes --show-labels


11. Deploy a sample application
Once AKS is running, deploy Nginx.
kubectl create deployment nginx \
  --image=nginx \
  --replicas=3


Expose the application:
kubectl expose deployment nginx \
  --type=LoadBalancer \
  --port=80


Check the service:
kubectl get services


Wait until an external IP is assigned, then access it through your browser.
12. Understand AKS node autoscaling








medium.com


There are two different scaling concepts:
Scaling	What it does
Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)	Increases or decreases pod replicas
Cluster Autoscaler	Increases or decreases worker nodes

For example:
- Initially: 2 user nodes.
- Application demand increases.
- Kubernetes schedules more pods.
- If existing nodes cannot accommodate them, Cluster Autoscaler can add nodes.
- The user node pool can scale up to 4 nodes.
The Terraform configuration enables the Cluster Autoscaler. HPA is a separate Kubernetes configuration that you can deploy later.
13. Destroy AKS infrastructure
When the training exercise is complete:
terraform destroy -var-file="terraform.tfvars"


This removes the AKS cluster and its Terraform-managed resource group.

Be aware that this deletes the workloads and infrastructure in the managed cluster. Check for persistent data and other resources before destroying it.

14. Important AKS Terraform concepts to teach
Terraform resource / property	Concept
azurerm_kubernetes_cluster	Creates AKS
default_node_pool	Creates the system node pool
azurerm_kubernetes_cluster_node_pool	Creates additional node pools
identity	Managed identity
network_profile	Kubernetes networking
auto_scaling_enabled	Enables node autoscaling
min_count	Minimum worker nodes
max_count	Maximum worker nodes
vm_size	Node VM specification
mode	System or User pool
lifecycle.ignore_changes	Prevents Terraform from reverting autoscaler-managed node counts
